> The attraction of NetHack, in my opinion, is especially that randomness,
> that holds surprises every time you play the game. There is a fine
> balance between randomness (RNG) and predictability (dungeon layout).

Surprises are possible also without randomness...  Think of playing 
chess...  In particular, playing it against a computer.

But I am not arguing at all against randomness.  I am arguing for 
increasing the number of ways to cope with randomness.

Nevertheless, I think this is a matter of taste, and we are assessing 
things differently here, so, yes, agreement is probably impossible.

For me the attraction of NH is not primarily its randomness, although I 
also like the surprise element.  The game would not be attractive for me 
at all, if random inevitable deaths appeared anytime in the late game. 
I would consider playing a waste of time.  If I have made a character 
survive 40000 turns, I want to know that I can ascend him, if I don't 
make a mistake.  This would not be the case, if one were completely at 
"the mercy of the rng".  I like NH precisely, because I know that I can 
cope with all the randomness it throws at me, once I have invested so 
much in my character.  I would not like it, if I died every so often on 
the Astral Plane.

Now I don't mind so much if a character dies that I have not yet 
invested much in.  Nevertheless, changes of the game that allow me to be 
less "at the mercy of the rng" (i.e. allow survival even in unfortunate 
situations, provided I just play well enough) would be welcome to me. 
That does not at all imply reducing randomness, just giving you more 
means to be resourceful.

> Everyone can get far by careful play.
> 
> Altar creation is completely unnecessary for this.

It would increase the percentage of characters that get far, unnecessary 
or not.  And it would do only for those that play carefully...

